## Changelog

- conjectured: LOAD-3 as originally written said default rollover rolls positive, zeroes negative, and carries the negative as visible debt with no auto-true-up.
  refuted_by: Those are two different rules. Zeroing a negative and carrying it as debt cannot both hold. Under zero-based budgeting a forgiven overspend is not free, so the deficit must be booked somewhere or the ledger leaks and the holding tripwire loses meaning.
  learned: The YNAB resolution is correct here. Default zeroes the envelope by booking the deficit into next period available-to-allocate, surfaced as a visible line in the wizard. Standing per-envelope debt is the roll_all override only.
  criterion_now: ISC-25, ISC-26, ISC-28.

- conjectured: LOAD-1 every-dollar rule should hard-block save whenever income does not equal the sum of allocations.
  refuted_by: With expected-versus-actual income (NEW-2) a hard per-save block fights reality. Mid-month edits, late actuals, and partial wizard sessions would all be blocked, and viewing must never be blocked.
  learned: Enforce the every-dollar rule at wizard completion against expected income, not on every intermediate save. Divergence of actual from expected is shown as a dashboard delta and prompts re-allocation, never blocks viewing.
  criterion_now: ISC-30, ISC-31, ISC-32.

- conjectured: The project was scoped single-user and local-only, with a dormant unused `owner` column as the sole concession to a possible future multi-principal need.
  refuted_by: Nick now wants the option to onboard another person with only their email and their PocketSmith dev key. A dormant column does not deliver that; tenant scoping has to be threaded through every read and write, auth has to resolve a user, and each user's dev key has to be isolated secret material. Retrofitting that into an event-sourced single-principal ledger after the fact is the exact painful migration the architect review warned against.
  learned: Build multi-tenant from migration one and run single-tenant locally. Promote `owner` to a real `users` table with `user_id` on every tenant table, scope every store query by the authenticated user, put auth and secrets behind interfaces, and make deployment a one-switch config. Local-first is preserved as the default; cloud becomes additive. This is the cheapest path to "ready to put on the cloud if I decide".
  criterion_now: ISC-47, ISC-48, ISC-49, ISC-50, ISC-51, ISC-52, ISC-53, ISC-54, ISC-55, ISC-56, ISC-57, ISC-58, ISC-59.

- conjectured: The MVP UI explicitly excluded a PWA; mobile was Tailscale-reachable desktop web only.
  refuted_by: Nick requires the surface be optimised for both web app and installed PWA, on his phone. A PWA needs HTTPS for service-worker registration, which a localhost or self-signed local deploy does not cleanly provide.
  learned: Promote the PWA into MVP scope (manifest, service worker, installable, offline shell). Solve local HTTPS with tailscale serve on a *.ts.net hostname, which issues a real cert. Keep offline transaction capture and any mobile write queue out of scope so the PWA stays read-and-allocate, not a sync-conflict surface.
  criterion_now: ISC-60, ISC-61, ISC-62, ISC-63, ISC-64.

- conjectured: The every-dollar rule should be enforced at wizard completion (block save until allocations balance expected income), and actual-vs-expected income variance should show as a dashboard delta line.
  refuted_by: Nick (2026-06-26): zero-based applies to the budget (the plan), not to actual cash flow. A non-zero-based budget should raise an alert, never block. And income variance should not live on a dashboard line; income is itself an envelope per source, and actual differing from budgeted is a surplus/deficit on that income envelope.
  learned: Two separable rules. (1) The zero-based check compares total spending allocations to total budgeted income and raises an alert on imbalance; saving and viewing are never blocked. (2) Income envelopes are first-class, one per source, each holding a budgeted amount and an actual balance; the variance is the surplus/deficit. This keeps the envelope metaphor whole on the income side and removes the only hard wall the design still had.
  criterion_now: ISC-30, ISC-31, ISC-32, ISC-65.

- conjectured: Mid-month allocation editing is just editing an envelope's budgeted number in place.
  refuted_by: Nick (2026-06-26): a reallocation must say where the money comes from and whether the change is one-off or ongoing. Extra income parked in Savings could be a one-time move of this month's surplus, or a permanent decision to expect more income and save more every month. Editing a single number cannot express either the source or the recurrence.
  learned: A reallocation is a move with three parts: destination, named source, and scope (this-month-only vs ongoing). The named source keeps the plan zero-based by construction. There is a recurring budget template behind the months; an ongoing change edits the template (so apply-last-month carries it forward), a this-month change is a period override that leaves the template untouched. Skipping the source is allowed but raises the zero-based alert, consistent with alert-not-block.
  criterion_now: ISC-66, ISC-67, ISC-68, ISC-69.

- conjectured: FLAG-1 default rollover zeroes an overspent envelope and books the deficit into the next period's available-to-allocate pool; carrying a per-envelope negative is the roll_all exception.
  refuted_by: Nick (2026-06-26): any envelope in surplus or deficit should either be balanced by a move (either direction) or carry over, and the carryover must be shown explicitly per envelope. Silently zeroing an envelope and docking a pooled number hides the deficit; Nick wants it visible on the envelope it belongs to.
  learned: Carryover in both directions is the default, shown as an explicit starting-carryover figure on every envelope row. Surplus and deficit both roll unless the user moves money to balance them. drain_to_zero (use-it-or-lose-it) and annual_reset remain per-envelope overrides. Each envelope row shows four figures: starting carryover, this-period allocation, total available (carryover + allocation), and left (total - spent). The deficit is still conserved (it is the negative carryover, not a leak), so ISC-28 holds. The Edit Budget wizard, not an inline baseline edit, is where the recurring template is changed.
  criterion_now: ISC-25, ISC-26, ISC-70, ISC-71, ISC-72.

